## ABSTRACT
Metal-insulator-metal (MIM) cavity as a lithography-free structure to control light transmission and reflection has great potential in the  field  of  optical  sensing.  However,  the  dense  top  metal  layer  of  the  MIM  prohibits  any  external  medium  from  entering  the dielectric  insulation  layer,  which  limits  the  application  of  the  cavity  in  the  sensing  field.  Herein,  we  demonstrate  a  series  of monolithic metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) based MIM cavities, which are treated by plasma etching to provide channels for chemical diffusion and to advance sensing. We modulate the bandwidth of the MIM filters by controlling the MOF thickness as insulator layers. Oxygen plasma-etching is applied to build channels on the top metal layer without altering their saturation and brightness  for  chemical  sensing  performance.  The  etching  time  regulates  the  number  and  size  of  channels  on  the  top  metal layer. Sensing behavior is demonstrated on the plasma-etched MOFs-based MIM cavity when external chemicals diffuse in the cavity. In addition, we generate patterned structure of the MOFs-based MIM cavity via plasma-mask method, which can transfer to different substrates and produce a controllable structure color change for chemical sensing. Our MIM cavity may promote the advancement  and  applications  of  structural  color  in  security  imaging,  color  display,  information  anticounterfeiting,  and  color printing.
## KEYWORDS
metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), spin-coating, metal-insulator-metal, filter, patterns, sensing
## Introduction
Metal-insulator-metal (MIM) cavity confines the electromagnetic field  in  a  small  volume  and  overcomes  the  diffraction  limit  by subwavelength interference of surface plasmon polaritons, which plays an important role in local phase modulation and wavefront manipulation  [1–3].  MIM  cavity  consists  of  two  metal  mirrors separated by dielectric insulator layers, which can be processed on a  large  area  for  practical  applications  by  fabrication  without lithography  [4, 5].  The  configuration  has  high  quality  factor efficient band-pass filtering and has been widely applied in spectral filters [6, 7], photoluminescence modification [8], nano-lasers [9], and sensors [10, 11]. Functionality of the MIM cavity depends on the  properties  of  insulators;  for  example,  reflection  color  can  be easily  controlled  depending  on  the  thickness  of  the  insulator [12–14]. Two main types of insulator materials have traditionally been used: inorganic materials and stimulus-responsive polymers. However, the former limits external intervention in the insulator region to dynamically modulate the reflection band [15–17], and the  latter  interferes  with  the  reflection  quality  due  to  uneven stretching  and  collapse  of  the  polymer  [18–20].  Moreover,  the dense  metal  layer  prevents  any  external  medium  from  entering into  the  insulator,  which  limits  their  dynamic  surface  color generation and optical sensing performance.
Metal-organic  frameworks  (MOFs)  provide  a  robust  and flexible platform for application in chemical sensing due to their large surface area and permanent porosity [21]. Especially, MOFs are highly attractive for gas storage or separation [22, 23], catalyzes [24, 25], optical devices [26, 27], and sensing [28, 29]. Reports of MOFs-based  sensors  have  been  constructed  by  depositing powders on substrates to monitor the refractive index dependent optical signals in response to volatile organic compounds (VOCs) [30–32]. Nevertheless, disordered grain arrangement of the MOFs powder  limits  the  coordination  between  optical  intensity  and optical  quality  [33–35].  So,  high-quality  MOFs  thin  films  are required to have sufficient physical contact with other architected materials  for  device  applications  [36].  By  using  a  layer-by-layer (LbL) growth strategy, we have developed monolithic MOFs films with  controllable  thicknesses  [37–40],  which  are  promising  for optical sensors due to the smaller light scattering. In addition, spincoating  [41]  was  proved  to  be  a  facile  approach  for  the  highthroughput  fabrication  of  homogeneous  and  highly  crystalline MOFs films.
Herein,  we  showed  a  series  of  monolithic  MOFs-based  MIM cavities with high color saturation and brightness, which achieved dynamic  optical  filtering  and  chemical  sensing  after  plasma etching treatment of the top Au layer. Monolithic HKUST-1 films were deposited on the bottom Au layer of the MIM cavities via LBL  spin-coating  method.  Bandwidth  of  the  monolithic  MOFsbased MIM cavities were tuned throughout the visible range by engineering the insulator thickness from 73 to $180~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . Plasmaetched  channels  were  produced  on  the  top  Au  layer  to  boost sensing performance. Fast response time of 4 s was observed for hexane  (Hex)  sensing  with  a  huge  wavelength  shift  of  about $40\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Patterned structure of the MOFs-based MIM cavity was fabricated by plasma-mask method, which could be transferred to various different subst- rates for visual sensing.
## 2    Results and discussion
Figure  2(a) showed  the  cross-sectional  scanning  electron microscopy  (SEM)  images  of  the  MOFs-based  MIM  cavities. Three-layered structure was observed with insulator thickness of $d=73$ ,  84,  126,  155,  168,  and $180\ \mathrm{nm}$ that  was  controlled  by varying  the  number  of  LBL  spin-coating  cycles  (30–80)  for  the MOF film growth. We further exploited how the reflection band of the MIM cavity depended on the MOFs layers’ thickness (Fig. $2(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{b}})_{,}^{\cdot}$ ). The reflection band of the series of $\operatorname{Au/MOFs/Au}$ cavities appeared  at $\lambda\ =\ 406.$ 433,  522,  562,  591,  and $650\ \mathrm{nm}$ ,  which correspondedtodifferentthicknessesoftheinsulatorlayertunedfrom73to $180~\mathrm{nm}$ . Consequently, optical images showed variable coloration from purple to red for the MOFs-based MIM cavity (insert of Fig. $2(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{b}})_{,}^{\cdot}$ ).  Also,  the  reflection  band  was  linearly  shifted  from  400  to $650~\mathrm{nm}$ as a function of the MOFs thickness, which covered the entire visible frequency domain (Fig. S3 in the ESM). Figure 2(c) depicted the reflection spectra obtained by finite-difference timedomain  (FDTD)  simulation  for  the  planar  MIM  cavity  with different  insulator  thicknesses  (details  in  the  ESM).  The  plane wave was normally incident onto the planar surface with arbitrary polarization and excited a wide light source from the incidence of the  cavity  to  reveal  bright  colors.  We  found  that  the  simulated reflection  agreed  well  with  the  experimental  results.  The coordinates distribution of the reflection band of the MIM cavity was  displayed  in  the  CIE  1931  chromaticity  diagram.  The  color gamut  of  the  reflection  band  covered $50\%$ of  the  standard  red, green,  and  blue  gamut  in  CIE  1931  color  space,  which demonstrated the wide-range color tuning of the MIM cavity (Fig. 2(d)).  Thus,  the  monolithic  MOFs-based  MIM  filter  enabled tunable bandwidth over broad ranges.
Reports of MIM cavity showed angle-dependent reflection due to  the  high  crystallinity  of  inorganic  materials  weakening  the interference of scattered light to coherent interference, which leads to an angle-dependent reflection [43, 44]. A higher index dielectric layer  could  mitigate  the  angle-dependence  of  colors  because  the refraction angle in a high-index film remained small over a wide range  of  incidence  angle  [45]. Figure  3 showed  the  angel dependent characterize of the MOFs-based MIM cavities. At an incidence  angle  of $0^{\circ}{\mathrm{:}}$ ,  reflection  bands  of  the  three  cavities appeared at $\lambda=410$ , 480, and $600\ \mathrm{nm}$ . When the incident angle was  changed  to $30^{\circ}$ and ${60}^{\circ}$ ,  these  reflection  band  remained unchanged,  which  indicated  that  the  angle  dependence  of reflection  in  our  monolithic  MOFs-based  MIM  cavity  can  be ignored because the HKUST-1 had higher refractive index [42]. The FDTD simulations of reflection spectra at different viewing angles  were  in  good  agreement  with  the  experimental  results, which  illustrated  the  angle-insensitive  characteristics  of  these structural colors (Fig. 3(c)).
The  typical  MIM  cavity  had  a  limit  for  sensing  application because the flat metal layer prohibits any external medium from entering the dielectric insulation layer. To address the challenge, we  tailored  channels  on  the  top  metal  layer  of  a  MOFs-based MIM cavity with orange reflection by plasma etching to advance chemical  sensing,  and  we  controlled  the  etching  time  to  avoid changing  the  reflected  color. Figure  4(a) shows  atomic  force microscopy (AFM) images of the MIM cavity surface after plasma etching for $0.5{-}2.0\ \mathrm{min}$ . For the pristine cavity, surface roughness of  the  top  gold  layer  was $4.9\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. S4  in  the  ESM).  After plasma etching for 0.5, 1.0, 1.5, and $2.0\mathrm{min}$ , surface roughness of the cavity increased to 5.6, 7.8, 9.1, and $10.4\mathrm{nm}$ , which indicated a rougher  surface  with  longer  plasma  etching  time.  Notably,  the yellow  color  was  defined  as  the  area  of  gold  film,  and  the  blue color was defined as the depth of etched channels in the MOFs layer  (insert  of Fig. 4(a)),  which  indicated  that  the  number  of channels  gradually  increased  with  increasing  the  etching  time. Figure  4(b) depicted  the  morphological  evolution  of  the  cavity surface after plasma etching, which developed more channels at a longer  etching  time.  XRD  pattern  indicated  that  the  crystal structure  of  MOFs  was  maintained  with  a  small  decrease  in intensity  after  plasma  etching  (Fig. S5  in  the  ESM).  Further,  we studied  the  ultraviolet–visible  spectroscopy  (UV–vis)  spectra  of the MOFs-based MIM cavity before and after plasma etching (Fig. $4(\mathrm{c}))$ . For the cavity after etching $0.5\mathrm{min}$ , the reflection band still appeared  at $\lambda=659\ \mathrm{nm}$ with  a  full  width  at  half  maximum (FWHM) of $192{\mathrm{nm}}$ , which had only a slight decrease in intensity. Similarly, the cavities with etching time of 1, 1.5, and 2 min kept their reflection band at $\lambda=644,$ 650, and $646~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . The reason is that the integrity of the surface gold film was damaged, which led to  the  reduction  in  the  effective  interference  between  two  gold layers  of  the  MIM  during  the  etching  process.  Thus,  plasma etching  on  the  MOFs-based  MIM  cavity  had  no  influence  on optical  filtering,  while  channels  were  developed  to  extend  the pathway for external chemical entry to advance sensing.
Dynamic sensing is an important way to analyze and evaluate specific  chemical  sensing  on  the  MOFs-based  MIM  cavity  [46]. Figure  S12  in  the  ESM  shows  dynamic  sensing  performance  of hexane on the $2.0\ \mathrm{min}$ plasma-etched MIM cavity. The primary reflection band (at $\lambda=607\mathrm{nm}$ ) experienced a blue-shift to $551\mathrm{nm}$ within $4\:s$ after exposure to hexane vapor. The large wavelength shift $(56\mathrm{nm})$ ) demonstrated high sensitivity of the cavity, in which the chemicals entered the MOFs layer through the plasma-etched channels  to  change  refractive  index  of  the  insulator.  Real-time monitoring of reflectance peaks was carried out to investigate the adsorption response of the MIM on chemical vapors (Fig. S13 in the  ESM).  The  response  time,  defined  as  the  differential absorption  reaching $95\%$ of  the  maximum  absorption,  was estimated  less  than $4.0s,$ which  showed  the  high  detection sensitivity and fast response time of the MIM cavity (Fig. S13(b) in the ESM).
To evaluate the dynamic ethanol sensing on the MOFs-based MIM cavity with different etching time, different concentration of ethanol was introduced to analyze their effect on spectrum shift (Fig. 6(a)).  Reflection  band  of  the $2.0\ \mathrm{min}$ plasma-etched  MIM cavity  appeared  at  around $607~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and  reached $642~\mathrm{nm}$ $\mathbf{\Delta}\cdot\Delta\lambda=$ $35~\mathrm{nm}$ ) by increasing the ethanol concentration from 0 to 1000 ppm, which showed a linear increase with a slope of 0.021 (Fig. 6(b)).  For  the  0.5,  1.0,  and $1.5\ \mathrm{min}$ etched  MIM  cavity,  the reflection band exhibited red-shift of $\Delta\lambda=14,24,$ and $30~\mathrm{nm}$ by increasing  ethanol  concentration  to $1000\ \mathrm{ppm}$ ,  which  showed  a linear increase with a slope of 0.009, 0.019, and 0.022, respectively. Notable, the pristine MOFs-based MIM cavity $(0.0\ \mathrm{min})$ showed no response of sensing. We found that the detection limit of the 0.5,  1.0,  1.5,  and $2.0\ \mathrm{min}$ etched  MIM  cavity  was  117.65,  52.63, 45.46,  and $47.62\textrm{\ p p m}$ ,  which  was  defined  as  the  analyte concentration  along  a $1\ \mathrm{nm}$ spectrum-shift  [39].  Separately,  we further  characterized  the  evolution  of  the  maximum  reflectance when  ethanol  vapors  were  exposed  to  the  plasma-etched  MIM cavity at different times of the sensing process. Figure $6(\mathrm{c})$ showed the evolution of average reflection from the uplifted state to the steady  state  by  introducing  different  concentrations  of  ethanol vapor on these plasma-etched MIM cavities. Reflectance shift of the $2.0\mathrm{min}$ etched MIM cavity varied from 2.6 to 3.8, 7.0, 8.4, and 9.0 upon exposure to 200, 400, 600, 800, and $1000\mathrm{ppm}$ of ethanol. Also,  the $\Delta R$ of  the $0.5{-}1.5~\mathrm{\min}$ etched  MIM  cavity  was characterized  and  showed  the  variation  from  1.0  to  22.9.  In contrast,  ΔR of  the  pristine  MOFs-based  MIM  cavity  did  not change  because  the  flat  metal  layer  prohibited  diffusion  of  any external medium to the insulator layer (Fig. S14 in the ESM). We found the replacement of carrier gas molecules (nitrogen) in the cavity with ethanol created the enhancement of reflectivity, which increased the average refractive index of HKUST-1 and led to a positive  response  of  ethanol  sensing  [47, 48]. Figure  6(d) demonstrated that the $0.5\mathrm{min}$ etched MIM cavity had the fastest response time $(t_{1}-t_{0})$ of $1.4\:s$ for the $200\ \mathrm{ppm}$ ethanol. Similarly, the 1.0, 1.5, $2.0\ \mathrm{min}$ etched MIM cavities had the fastest response time of 5.8, 10.6, and $1.8~\mathsf{s},$ respectively. Thus, the plasma-etched MOFs-based  MIM  cavity  significantly  advanced  the  detection limit for chemical sensing.
To demonstrate the vivid chemical-responsive structural colors, we fabricated a patterned structure of the monolithic MOFs-based MIM  cavity  by  using  plasma-mask  method  (Fig. 7). Figure  7(a) showed schematic illustration of a 2D multiplexing imaging with designable pattern to add a dynamic tuning function. In brief, we prepared the two types of imaging regions that were colored by two kinds of unit pixels. Region 1 was color-invariant pixels that made up of Au/MOFs cavity, and region 2 was color-responsive pixels that made up of Au/MOFs/Au cavity. MOF thickness was about $73\mathrm{nm}$ for both of the above cavities. Figure 7(b) showed the change  of  reflection  spectra  and  reflected  color  on  patterned structure of the MIM cavity for hexene and ethanol sensing. The reflection  band  of  the  school  logo  region  on  the  pristine  MIM cavity appeared at $\lambda=435~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , which shifted to $\lambda=406~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and $\lambda=458\mathrm{nm}$ by immersing in hexane and ethanol, respectively. The corresponding reflected color of the patterned MIM changed from blue to purple and cyan, while the yellow background area showed no resonant peak in the visible region due to the lack of top Au layer (insert of Fig. $7(\mathrm{b})\dot{}$ ). Thus, the patterned MOFs-based MIM cavity  had  potential  in  dynamic  multicolor  imaging  and  visible sensing.
In  addition,  we  acquired  transferable  patterns  of  the  MOFsbased  MIM  cavity  on  various  substrates  such  as  scotch  tape, curved  glass,  and  polyethylene  terephthalate.  The  robust  and steerable patterned structure of the MIM cavity was transferred to transparent and curved substrates by peeling and attaching (Fig. 8(a)). Also, the transferred pattern of MOFs-based MIM cavity on the scotch tape showed function of sensing, which was originally orange and turned to purple and red by immersing in hexane and ethanol  (Fig. 8(b)). Figure  8(c) showed  the  recoverability  of  the MOFs-based  MIM  cavity  transferred  on  a  flexible  polyethylene terephthalate substrate. We found the MOFs-based cavity had no obvious color change after stretching for different strains of $15\%$ $30\%$ ,  and $45\%$ ,  indicating  that  the  cavity  showed  good stretchability and repeatability for largescale flexible color display.
## 3 Conclusions
In summary, we constructed monolithic MOFs-based MIM cavity with  plasma-etched  channel  to  improve  chemical  sensing.  The MIM cavity was fabricated by sandwiching monoclinic MOFs thin film  generated  by  optimized  LBL  spin-coating  method  between the gold layers. Programmable tuning of the cavity bandwidth was achieved  for  large  visible  range  color  filtering  by  changing  the thickness of monolithic MOFs film. FDTD simulation proved that the  thickness  of  MOFs  can  modulate  the  reflection  band  of  the MIM cavity. Plasma etching on the MIM cavity allowed external chemical diffusion, which realized the breakthrough enhancement of  detection  sensitivity.  In  addition,  optimization  of  the  plasma etching time was demonstrated to promote the chemical sensing performance.  Further,  we  realized  patterned  structure  of  the MOFs-based  MIM  cavity  on  various  different  substrates  and visualized  chromaticity  sensing  on  transferable  substrate.  Our monolithic MOFs-based MIM cavity has the potential to foldable, transportable,  and  energy-efficient  electronic  readers.  Also,  the plasma-etching  strategy  facilitates  the  development  of  in-suit engineer channels on optical cavities to advance chemical sensing.
